1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,933
Everything in this section so far has been a habit or a principle.

2
00:00:03,933 --> 00:00:08,439
This lecture turns it into a document, because a principle you cannot point to is a

3
00:00:08,439 --> 00:00:11,044
principle you will skip under deadline pressure.

4
00:00:11,044 --> 00:00:15,496
The job description is the artifact that makes the four onboarding habits from 1.1

5
00:00:15,496 --> 00:00:19,676
impossible to shortcut, because you cannot fill it in without answering them.

6
00:00:19,676 --> 00:00:22,824
Be honest about what deadline pressure does to principles.

7
00:00:22,824 --> 00:00:27,384
In a calm week you would never grant broad access; in the week the report is due and

8
00:00:27,384 --> 00:00:31,944
the tool is nearly working, "connect everything and tidy it later" feels reasonable,

9
00:00:31,944 --> 00:00:33,138
and later never comes.

10
00:00:33,138 --> 00:00:34,984
The document exists for that week.

11
00:00:34,984 --> 00:00:39,436
It takes about half an hour the first time and closer to ten minutes once you have

12
00:00:39,436 --> 00:00:43,507
done one, and it is the difference between your judgement being a thing you

13
00:00:43,507 --> 00:00:47,850
exercised once, in writing, and a thing you have to re-argue with yourself every

14
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:49,533
time a shortcut looks tempting.

15
00:00:50,083 --> 00:00:54,876
Notice question two asks for the exact folder, not "my email." Vague scope is where

16
00:00:54,876 --> 00:00:57,937
blast radius quietly grows past what anyone intended.

17
00:00:57,937 --> 00:01:02,441
Question four is the one people most often leave blank, and it is the one that

18
00:01:02,441 --> 00:01:07,119
matters most: a ceiling only works if you wrote it down before you needed it, not

19
00:01:07,119 --> 00:01:07,465
after.

20
00:01:07,465 --> 00:01:09,660
Question one has a test built into it.

21
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:14,338
If your one sentence needs the word "and", you are describing two agents, and the

22
00:01:14,338 --> 00:01:19,131
course's advice is always the same: build the first half, let the second half wait.

23
00:01:19,131 --> 00:01:23,866
"Flags urgent email and drafts replies" is two jobs with two different risk levels

24
00:01:23,866 --> 00:01:25,079
wearing one sentence.

25
00:01:25,079 --> 00:01:29,641
Question five is where you stop being a form filler and start being the expert.

26
00:01:29,641 --> 00:01:33,626
Nobody else in your company can write what urgent means on your desk.

27
00:01:33,626 --> 00:01:38,304
That definition, in plain English, is the single highest value paragraph you will

28
00:01:38,304 --> 00:01:42,751
produce in this entire course, because it is the paragraph the agent actually

29
00:01:42,751 --> 00:01:44,599
reasons from every time it runs.

30
00:01:45,149 --> 00:01:47,906
Read that back and notice how ordinary it sounds.

31
00:01:47,906 --> 00:01:48,919
That is the point.

32
00:01:48,919 --> 00:01:53,421
A good job description is not clever, it is specific enough that someone else in

33
00:01:53,421 --> 00:01:58,036
your office could read it and know exactly what the agent is and is not allowed to

34
00:01:58,036 --> 00:01:59,330
do, without asking you.

35
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:02,200
Three of Priya's answers are worth slowing down on.

36
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:06,871
Read only, for a triage agent, is not timidity: the job is to point, and a pointing

37
00:02:06,871 --> 00:02:11,542
agent that cannot write anything has a blast radius close to zero, which is exactly

38
00:02:11,542 --> 00:02:14,074
what you want from a first hire's first week.

39
00:02:14,074 --> 00:02:18,633
The ceiling, never marks anything as resolved, is aimed at a specific failure: an

40
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:23,304
agent that closes a query wrongly makes work disappear, and disappeared work is the

41
00:02:23,304 --> 00:02:25,498
silent kind of damage from lecture 1.3.

42
00:02:25,498 --> 00:02:27,581
Flagging wrongly just costs a glance.

43
00:02:27,581 --> 00:02:31,126
And the sign off line introduces Marcus Webb, Priya's director.

44
00:02:31,126 --> 00:02:35,403
He does not appear at the trial, because the trial is Priya checking her own

45
00:02:35,403 --> 00:02:36,022
delegation.

46
00:02:36,022 --> 00:02:40,411
He appears at Review, because that is the rung where the agent's output starts

47
00:02:40,411 --> 00:02:45,139
reaching work other people depend on, and that is the moment a boss is entitled to a

48
00:02:45,139 --> 00:02:45,364
say.

49
00:02:45,364 --> 00:02:49,922
Matching the sign off to the rung is what makes this a real document instead of a

50
00:02:49,922 --> 00:02:50,203
form.

51
00:02:50,753 --> 00:02:53,704
Nothing on this page is new, and that is its strength.

52
00:02:53,704 --> 00:02:58,240
If any row feels unfamiliar, the middle column says which lecture to rewatch before

53
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:59,059
you fill it in.

54
00:02:59,059 --> 00:03:03,485
The right column is the honest reason each question earns its place: every one of

55
00:03:03,485 --> 00:03:08,021
them is a specific, boring failure that has already cost someone real money, folded

56
00:03:08,021 --> 00:03:10,316
down into one line you answer in a minute.

57
00:03:10,866 --> 00:03:14,927
Here is a test that works for every row on the left: could you tell, on Friday,

58
00:03:14,927 --> 00:03:16,469
whether the job was done well?

59
00:03:16,469 --> 00:03:20,736
"Handle my email" cannot be checked, so it cannot be trialled, so it can never earn

60
00:03:20,736 --> 00:03:21,044
trust.

61
00:03:21,044 --> 00:03:25,259
Each version on the right can be marked right or wrong against reality, one output

62
00:03:25,259 --> 00:03:25,773
at a time.

63
00:03:25,773 --> 00:03:27,212
That is not a writing style.

64
00:03:27,212 --> 00:03:31,273
It is the property that makes the whole trust ladder possible, and when a trial

65
00:03:31,273 --> 00:03:35,283
feels unrunnable, the fault is almost always here, in the sentence, not in the

66
00:03:35,283 --> 00:03:35,591
agent.

67
00:03:36,141 --> 00:03:38,625
What if you genuinely cannot name a ceiling?

68
00:03:38,625 --> 00:03:40,092
Treat that as information.

69
00:03:40,092 --> 00:03:44,776
It usually means the task involves a judgement you have not yet put into words, and

70
00:03:44,776 --> 00:03:47,993
an agent is about to exercise that judgement without you.

71
00:03:47,993 --> 00:03:52,621
Shrink the job until a ceiling becomes nameable: narrower folder, draft instead of

72
00:03:52,621 --> 00:03:54,145
send, flag instead of file.

73
00:03:54,145 --> 00:03:58,604
If it still will not shrink, you may be holding one of the tasks Section 9 says

74
00:03:58,604 --> 00:04:02,498
should stay human, and finding that out now, on paper, costs nothing.

75
00:04:02,498 --> 00:04:04,191
Keep this filled-in page open.

76
00:04:04,191 --> 00:04:08,932
Section 3 builds your first agent from scratch against exactly this document, so the

77
00:04:08,932 --> 00:04:11,923
scope and ceiling you just wrote are not theoretical.

78
00:04:11,923 --> 00:04:13,447
They are what you build to.
